# ── 2026-09-06 OWNERSHIP AND DROP LATTICE (459 cells, generator tests/lattice/drop) ──
# Read on the binary of build 10f55b7c2fa44145 43 (HEAD 69210911f): every cell
# compiled, linked, RUN, and its DESTRUCTOR COUNT read off a raw-pointer counter —
# a count for "how many", a `*c = *c * 10 + v` sequence counter for "in what order".
# Six roots, named by symbol in each program's header, not by spelling.

# (delete the row, decrement `# TOTAL`, land the program as a pass/fail fixture,
# all in the same commit) or was never real. Before this file existed the queue
# lived in prose and failed twice in four days: one defect was recorded under an
# innocent program, and a "five owner fixtures wall this" verdict was carried six
# rounds after four of the five had been repaired.
#
# ⚠ THE RECORDED BEHAVIOUR IS A MEASUREMENT WITH A DATE. It was read on the
# binary of 2026-09-04 (HEAD ac27d1a99): compiled, linked, RUN, exit code and
# stdout read, valgrind where a heap error was possible. A row's wrong behaviour
# was never copied from a description — three of the five 2026-09-04 tier-1
# candidates and the "type with both drops runs the inherent one" candidate did
# not survive re-running and are not here.
#
# Format:  <id> <tier> <path relative to the repo root, no .logos> <observed...>
#
# `observed` is the WRONG behaviour today, machine-checkable, one of:
#   admits          compiles with no diagnostic; rustc refuses the program
#   refuses         does not compile clean (a diagnostic, or the compiler died);
#                   the program is legal Rust
#   run <rc>        compiles clean, links, and the binary exits <rc> (wrong)
#   diag <text...>  refused, and stderr contains <text> — the program is
#                   illegal, the SENTENCE is the defect
# The reader compiles the way the pass tier does (LOGOS_VERIFY_LAYOUT=1), so a
# compiler crash the fixture corpus would meet is a `refuses` here.
#
# `tier`:  1 memory corruption / wrong code runs      2 a class is not checked
#          3 legal Rust refused                        4 diagnostics and hygiene
#

